===== HOW TO AVOID PIXELISATION (BLURRY/BLOCKY DRAWINGS) =====

WHAT IS PIXELISATION?
- Your drawing looks blurry, blocky, or "pixelated"
- Text is hard to read
- Circles look like squares with jagged edges
- Lines look chunky instead of smooth

WHY DOES THIS HAPPEN?
Think of it like trying to display a huge detailed poster on a tiny TV screen:
- Your canvas is big (like a 2048x2048 detailed drawing)
- But your 3D plane mesh is tiny (like a postage stamp)
- The computer has to "squish" your big drawing to fit the tiny plane
- All that detail gets compressed and looks pixelated

THE REAL PROBLEM:
Your 3D plane mesh is TOO SMALL for your canvas size!

HOW TO FIX IT:

1. MAKE YOUR 3D PLANE BIGGER (BEST FIX)
   - In Wonderland Editor, select your plane object
   - Increase the Scale values (try 2, 3, 4, or even 10)
   - This makes your plane bigger so the canvas detail shows properly
   - IN CODE: You can also scale it with transform.scaling

2. OR MAKE YOUR CANVAS SMALLER (IF PLANE MUST STAY SMALL)
   - If your plane needs to stay tiny, reduce canvas size
   - Instead of 1024x1024, try 256x256 or 512x512
   - Less detail, but it will look cleaner on the small plane
   - IN CODE: Change canvasWidth and canvasHeight properties

3. MATCH CANVAS SIZE TO PLANE SIZE
   - Big plane = big canvas (1024x1024 or 2048x2048)
   - Small plane = small canvas (256x256 or 512x512)
   - Medium plane = medium canvas (512x512 or 1024x1024)
   - They should be proportional to each other

4. USE "POWER OF 2" SIZES
   - Use sizes like: 256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096
   - Don't use: 300, 500, 1000, 1500
   - Computers handle power-of-2 sizes much better
   - They're faster and look better

5. KEEP CANVAS AND PLANE THE SAME SHAPE
   - If your canvas is 512x512 (square)...
   - Make sure your 3D plane is also square shaped
   - If your plane is rectangular, use rectangular canvas (like 1024x512)
   - Mismatched shapes cause stretching and distortion

6. MAKE TEXT AND LINES BIGGER FOR SMALL PLANES
   - If you must keep the plane small, make elements bigger
   - Use 24px+ fonts instead of 12px
   - Use 3-5 pixel thick lines instead of 1 pixel
   - Bigger elements survive the compression better

REAL WORLD EXAMPLE:
Making a readable sign in VR:

WRONG WAY:
- Canvas: 2048x2048 pixels (huge detail)
- 3D Plane: Scale 0.1, 0.1, 1 (tiny postage stamp)
- Result: All that detail gets squished = pixelated mess

RIGHT WAY OPTION 1 (Make plane bigger):
- Canvas: 2048x2048 pixels (huge detail)
- 3D Plane: Scale 5, 5, 1 (big billboard size)
- Result: Crystal clear, all detail visible

RIGHT WAY OPTION 2 (Make canvas smaller):
- Canvas: 512x512 pixels (moderate detail)
- 3D Plane: Scale 0.1, 0.1, 1 (tiny postage stamp)
- Result: Clean but less detailed

PERFORMANCE vs QUALITY BALANCE:

FOR SMALL UI ELEMENTS:
- Small plane scale (0.5, 0.5, 1)
- Small canvas (256x256 or 512x512)
- Keep it fast and clean

FOR LARGE DISPLAYS/BILLBOARDS:
- Large plane scale (5, 5, 1 or bigger)
- Large canvas (1024x1024 or 2048x2048)
- Maximum quality for big viewing

FOR MEDIUM INTERACTIVE PANELS:
- Medium plane scale (2, 2, 1)
- Medium canvas (1024x1024)
- Good balance of quality and performance

QUICK FIXES CHECKLIST:
□ First try: Double your plane's scale values
□ If plane must stay small: Halve your canvas size
□ Use power-of-2 canvas dimensions only
□ Match canvas shape to plane shape (both square or both rectangle)
□ Make fonts and lines bigger if plane is small
□ Test from the distance people will actually view it

REMEMBER:
- Big plane = can show big canvas detail clearly
- Small plane = needs small canvas to avoid pixelation
- The key is matching them properly
- Start with plane size, then choose appropriate canvas size

SIMPLE RULE OF THUMB:
If your drawing looks pixelated, your plane is probably too small for your canvas size. 
Make the plane bigger, or make the canvas smaller!

IN WONDERLAND EDITOR:
1. Select your plane object
2. In the Transform section, try Scale: 2, 2, 1
3. If still pixelated, try Scale: 4, 4, 1
4. Keep increasing until it looks good
5. Or reduce canvas size in the component properties